WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.657
An antibody-drug conjugate is a chemical system. Tumour binding

00:00:04.657 --> 00:00:08.764
is only one step in an exposure and release problem.

00:00:13.467 --> 00:00:17.869
Stable, cleavable and non-cleavable linkers change what escapes the carrier,

00:00:17.869 --> 00:00:22.270
how much reaches other tissues and how a tumour is exposed.

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:32.046
Drug-to-antibody ratio influences distribution, heterogeneity and pharmacokinetics.

00:00:32.046 --> 00:00:36.792
Higher loading is not automatically more precise.

00:00:42.092 --> 00:00:46.900
Even a stable linker may deliver a drug class

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:51.709
associated with specific toxicities, including serious complications outside the tumour.

00:00:57.008 --> 00:01:01.374
Permeable payloads may help reach heterogeneous tumours, but

00:01:01.374 --> 00:01:05.740
surrounding normal tissue and exposure constrain the apparent advantage.

00:01:10.675 --> 00:01:15.115
The article argues that conjugation chemistry, payload choice and

00:01:15.115 --> 00:01:19.556
predictive toxicology belong together from the start of development.
